title: Genesis 11 – The Tower of Babel
description: A study in Genesis 11: the tower of Babel, God scattering the nations, the generations of Shem to Abram, and how God judges nations and heals their land.
output: lessons/genesis/genesis-11-tower-of-babel.html

=== Introduction
--- The Tower of Babel [title]
overline: A study in Genesis 11
sub: A tower to Heaven, the nations scattered, and the generations of Shem to Abram.
refs: Genesis 11:1-9
hint: Use the arrow keys or swipe to move. Tap any reference to read the verse.

=== A tower to Heaven
--- One language
scripture: Genesis 11:1-4 | mark: let us make us a name, lest we be scattered abroad
p: About 100 years after the flood, in the year of Peleg’s birth (*for in his days was the earth divided*), the people imagined a vain thing. A tower to Heaven. That they could work their way to Heaven and be like God. [[Genesis 10:25]] [[Genesis 3:5]]

--- Let us go down
heading: “Let us go down”
scripture: Genesis 11:5-9 | mark: Go to, let us go down; So the LORD scattered them abroad
p: The {Lord} God, the Father, says “*let us go down*…” And they scattered the people and confused their language. [[Genesis 1:26]]

--- We are meant to be in nations
lead: We are meant to be in nations. Nations by clan. By blood.
panel-warn: Not one world government | Full of vanity, *making a name for ourselves, lest we be scattered abroad upon the face of the whole earth.*
panel-warn: Not paper and statutes | Not even a civic nationalism of paper and statutes that erases who we are.
refs: Genesis 10:5; Acts 17:26

--- One in Christ
heading: The unity God gives
big: John 17:20-23 | mark: That they all may be one; as thou, Father, art in me, and I in thee
center: Babel sought to be one by its own works, to make a name. Christ prays that those who believe on Him through the word be one in the Father and the Son.

=== Nimrod
--- A mighty hunter before the Lord
heading: A mighty hunter before the {Lord}
scripture: Genesis 10:8-10 | mark: the beginning of his kingdom was Babel
p: Nimrod gets a bad rap. Having founded Babel and being a mighty man, a hunter before the {Lord}, it is assumed he’s the impetus for the construction of the tower.
- Most of the assumption is based on his proximity, his greatness, being a *gibbor* or mighty man, and his lineage forming the great cities listed in Genesis 10: Babel, Erech, Accad and Calneh, in the land of Shinar.
- Some translate his name as “deserter,” others “he rebelled,” and build an entire story around him, the tower, and great wickedness against God.
callout-honest: Yet scripture doesn’t give a negative accounting of him.

=== Shem to Abram
--- The generations of Shem
scripture: Genesis 11:10-13
p: Then we are given the generations of Shem, leading to Terah, father of Abram and grandfather of Lot. [[Genesis 11:27]]

--- Where and when we are
lead: The genealogy of man after the flood is again given to establish where and when we are for the next phase of the story: the origins and chronology of the people God dealt with.
tile: Genesis 5 | Adam to Noah
tile: Genesis 11 | Noah to Abram
tile: Matthew 1, Luke 3 | To Jesus Christ
p: In like manner, the Gospels have similar genealogies, that we have a lineage and time frame from Adam to Jesus’ earthly incarnation. Many overlook this as simply a history or chronological device, or try to extract much deeper meaning from it than is there, often at the expense of believing it literally, especially the timing of years. [[Luke 3:34-38]]

--- Life spans shrinking
scripture: Genesis 11:24-26
stat: 600 | years, Shem [[Genesis 11:10-11]]
stat: 205 | years, Terah [[Genesis 11:32]]
stat: 175 | years, Abraham [[Genesis 25:7]]
p: The life spans of man are shrinking as we go from Shem to Abram, approaching the hundred and twenty years God decided back in Genesis 6. A shadow of the 900 years most of the earlier patriarchs lived to. [[Genesis 6:3]]

=== God judges nations
--- No respecter of persons
lead: God is not a respecter of persons, the Bible says repeatedly. He does not care if you are great or small, rich or poor, man or woman, Greek or Jew, where you’re from or who your parents are.
big: Acts 10:34-35
center: He gives us all the same offer of salvation and adoption in Christ. As individuals, we have free will. [[Galatians 3:28]]

--- But God does judge nations
lead: God does judge nations. He favors some over others at times, and destroys many who go too far astray.
- He curses the land stained by the blood of violence, sodomy, fornication. [[Numbers 35:33]] [[Leviticus 18:24-25]]
- Of people, collectively, to the fourth generation. [[Exodus 34:7]]
callout: **We are to know the judgment of God and emulate it.** Not being a respecter of persons, giving the Gospel to whomever we meet, but judging ungodly nations for what they are. Including our own.

--- Scattered as judgment
lead: As at Babel, scattering is God’s judgment on a people who forsake Him.
scripture: Leviticus 26:33
scripture: Deuteronomy 4:26-27 | mark: the LORD shall scatter you among the nations
refs: Ezekiel 36:19-23; Jeremiah 3:13; Lamentations 4:16

--- Yet a remnant
scripture: Ezekiel 6:8 | mark: Yet will I leave a remnant
scripture: Romans 11:5-8 | mark: a remnant according to the election of grace
center: The remnant is by grace, not works. The rest were blinded.

--- Reprobate silver
big: Jeremiah 6:30
p: Speaking of the Jews who would reject the coming Messiah, Romans 1 applies to them as it applies to all that go the way of the Pharaoh. Because they did not like to retain God in their knowledge, “He hath blinded their eyes, and hardened their heart.” By their individual free will. Collectively judged. [[John 12:40]] [[Romans 1:28]] [[1 Thessalonians 2:14-16]]
note: See [[lesson:topic-reprobate|The Reprobate Mind]] for the whole doctrine.

=== Heal their land
--- Not “doing nothing”
lead: Unsaved men often criticize born again believers as “doing nothing,” as weak, afraid or cowardly, because we aren’t running around in panic, getting sucked into reactionary spirals by the provocations of the rulers of the darkness of this world.
p: They rail and demand we join seditions and take vengeance for ourselves. They fail to notice that the more they do this, the worse the situation becomes for everyone. This frustration, doubt and impatience with God can strike even believing Christians. [[Romans 12:19]]

--- If my people
big: 2 Chronicles 7:14
center: God is clear on how we save our nation and get His blessings for our people.

--- The hard work
- Have faith. Obey the commandments. As much as possible, live peaceably among men. [[Romans 12:18]]
- Be a godly husband, raise godly children, and share the gospel, winning souls.
- Give comfort to those mourning, be a good judge for the fatherless and true widows. [[James 1:27]]
callout: This is not “doing nothing.” It is hard work, or else it wouldn’t be so difficult to get people to do it. Like most effective things, it requires discipline, motivation and perseverance. It’s easier to express impotent outrage online.

--- We wrestle not against flesh and blood
big: Ephesians 6:12
p: The frustrations and provocations assailing us are how Satan tempts us, so he can accuse the saints before God: to make us sin, so that we can be persecuted for those sins by the powers that be. They avoid persecuting believers for His name’s sake as much as possible, because when they clearly persecute us for our faith, directly, they lose. [[Revelation 12:10]] [[1 Peter 4:14-16]]
center: As a people, if Christians turn towards God and obey Him, fearful of nothing except God, He will hear us, heal our land, and bring blessings to all our nation, believers or not.

--- So stop doing nothing and believe [title]
overline: Genesis 11
heading: Believe
p: Just being loving and kind, while maintaining the separation and sanctification God asks of us, is a dissident, revolutionary act. When we live blamelessly, it heaps coals of fire upon their heads. But that all requires our faith. So stop doing nothing and believe. [[Romans 12:20-21]]

=== Close
--- The tower and the nations [title]
overline: Genesis 11
point: Babel was works religion | A tower to Heaven, to make a name, that they might be like God.
point: God scattered them | We are meant to be in nations, and God judges nations.
point: Shem to Abram | The genealogy sets where and when we are, literally, on the way to Christ.
point: If my people | Faith, obedience, godly homes and the gospel heal a land.

